      "title": "Ranged edges, joined into one wide frame",
      "description": "**One frame per edge plus one `joinByField` \u2014 the documented Prometheus recipe.**\n\nThe same four series as the time series above. A `Time series` response is *many* frames, and the reader does collect every frame that looks like edges \u2014 but **not one of these frames looks like edges**. Their labels are the fixture's own, not `source`/`target`, and every value field is called `Value`, which has no separator to split on. Left alone this panel draws nothing at all. `joinByField` on `Time` widens them, and `joinDataFrames` renames each `Value` field to its **frame name** \u2014 which is the rendered legend format, i.e. the edge id, and the only endpoint carrier this response has.\n\nThat is the whole conversion for Prometheus and Loki: `format: Time series`, `legendFormat: {{client}}-->{{server}}`, one join, no SQL Expressions. This panel is the single-row version; the dashboard **ECharts Relations \u2014 Prometheus & Loki sources** (`observability-sources.json`) starts from the frame shapes those datasources really return and carries the whole chain, including the label-carrier question. See also [docs/relations-data-sources.md](../../../docs/relations-data-sources.md).\n\n`a-->c` keeps its fixed colour through the join, and each edge's value is `calcs[0]` over the range \u2014 `b-->d` stays hidden by the same `custom.hideFrom` override the time series uses.",
